course: dictionary
lesson: 5061
title: Firm words: insist
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To insist means to say something firmly, so that people know you mean it. You can insist that something must happen, or insist that something is true. «She insisted on coming to the park, even though it was raining.» Insisted shows that she did not give up her plan.
explain: The word often comes with a small pattern. Insist on is followed by a word ending in -ing, as in «insist on washing your hands». Insist that is followed by a full idea, as in «He insisted that he was innocent». Check the sentence: does it sound like someone holding firm to their point?
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Alice insists she is a little girl» tells you Alice is sure of it, even when the Pigeon doubts her. A parent who says «I insist you wear a helmet» is not asking politely; the firm tone and the word insist show that it is a rule.
fact: To insist means to say something firmly, and not accept «no».
fact: To insist also means to say that something is true, even when others do not believe you.
fact: Insist on is followed by an -ing word, as in «insist on coming».
fact: Insist that is followed by a full idea, as in «insist that you are right».
fact: «I insist!» can be a polite phrase when you kindly offer something, as in «Have the last biscuit, I insist!»
exercise: «She insisted on coming to the park, even though it was raining.» What does insisted mean here? | She was too tired to speak | She was sorry about the rain | *She said firmly that she must go, and would not take no for an answer | She ran away from the park
exercise: Some people do not shake hands, and the text says you must respect that without «insisting». What should you do? | Insist they shake hands, to be polite | Tell them they are wrong | Shout until they agree | *Smile or nod instead
exercise: «Alice insists she is a little girl.» What does insists suggest? | *She is sure of it, even though others may doubt her | She has forgotten who she is | She is asking nicely for a turn | She is happy to be a serpent
test: Mum says, «I insist you wear a helmet.» What does insist show here? | *She firmly means it, and it is a rule | She is offering you a sweet | She is bored of you | She is asking you to think about it
test: Grandma insists ___ wearing warm «qiūkù» from October to May. | to | by | *on | at
exam: «He insisted that he was innocent.» What does insisted that he was innocent mean? | He admitted he had done wrong | *He said firmly that he was not guilty, and did not change his story | He ran away from the police | He was very hungry
